WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.762
Welcome to Grade 3 English, Unit 3: Time and Daily Life.

2
00:00:03.842 --> 00:00:07.742
In this unit you will learn all about time and daily life.

3
00:00:07.822 --> 00:00:12.270
You will name the twelve months
of the year and put them in order.

4
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.554
You will talk about your daily routine
— what you do at each hour of the day.

5
00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:24.415
You will learn to talk about the past (what already
happened), the present (what happens every day),

6
00:00:24.495 --> 00:00:27.091
and the future (what will happen soon).

7
00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:33.746
By the end, you will be able to make your own
calendar, just like Amal does in this unit’s story,

8
00:00:33.826 --> 00:00:35.599
“The Calendar on the Wall”.

9
00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:41.362
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:48.799
By the end of this unit, you will be able to name
and spell the twelve months of the year, in order.

11
00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:54.080
You will also talk about your daily
routine using the hours of the day.

12
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.509
You will also use the Present Simple
to talk about what you do every day.

13
00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:02.260
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:08.025
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
January, February, March, April, May, June.

15
00:01:08.105 --> 00:01:13.595
Listen to each word, look at what it means,
and try to use it when you talk and write.

16
00:01:13.675 --> 00:01:18.563
The reading sequence includes My Day,
Hour by Hour and The Twelve Months.

17
00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:23.326
Read slowly, look at the pictures,
and find the big idea in each text.

18
00:01:23.406 --> 00:01:27.409
When you answer a question, point to the words that show it.

19
00:01:27.489 --> 00:01:30.171
The first language focus is Present Simple.

20
00:01:30.251 --> 00:01:37.429
We use the Present Simple to talk about things that happen
again and again, and about facts that are always true.

21
00:01:37.509 --> 00:01:39.099
It is the “routine tense”.

22
00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:44.366
When we say “She marks the calendar every
morning,” we mean she does it regularly.

23
00:01:44.446 --> 00:01:47.320
This helps us describe our daily life clearly.

24
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.495
Look at the examples, then try
the pattern in your own sentences.

25
00:01:51.575 --> 00:01:55.553
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1 — My Daily Routine.

26
00:01:55.633 --> 00:01:58.879
Say your ideas clearly and listen to your partner.

27
00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:05.663
In writing, you will draw, trace, copy or write,
then check your work with a grown-up or the AI tutor.

28
00:02:05.743 --> 00:02:07.226
You are ready to begin.

29
00:02:07.306 --> 00:02:13.815
Start with the words, then the reading and the
questions, then the grammar, speaking and writing.

30
00:02:13.895 --> 00:02:20.540
Ask the AI tutor for help any time, and bring your
questions to a live session too if you join one.
